# The whole point of the picture is that only one box changes. The runtime, the app and the
# installer are the ones a reader already has, and the swapped file is a single download.

title Swapping the JIT under a runtime you did not build

size 980 570

box app 40 70 260 100 "your app" "an ordinary dll, built" "by an ordinary SDK"
box host 350 70 280 100 "stock release runtime" "installed with one command," "nothing built from source"

box stock 40 240 260 110 "libclrjit, shipped" "the release JIT, next to" "the runtime. No dump switches"
box checked 350 240 280 110 "libclrjit_checked, added" "the checked JIT for the same" "release branch, one download"

box var 680 240 260 110 "DOTNET_JitName" "names the file to load," "so nothing is overwritten"

box dump 350 430 280 100 "DOTNET_JitDump works" "four thousand lines of phases" "for one named method"

note cost 680 430 260 100 "about twice the JIT time" "and no change to the code" "the JIT produces"

arrow app host
arrow host stock
arrow host checked
arrow var checked
arrow checked dump
